<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Despotate of Arta</h1><p class="st-meta">1358 CE – 1416 CE (approx.) · capital Arta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albanian despotate in southern Epirus, ruled by the Losha and Shpata clans from Arta until Carlo Tocco took the city in 1416.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1358–1359 Albanian clans defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-orsini">Nikephoros II Orsini</a>, the last Orsini despot, and divided southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-losha">Peter Losha</a> took Arta and was recognised as despot by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-uros">Simeon Uroš</a>, the Serbian ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a> held the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto">Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto</a> to the south.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The despots relied on Albanian clans who had moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, ruling a Greek town population around Arta. They fought the Serbian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-preljubovic">Thomas Preljubović</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esau-de-buondelmonti">Esau de' Buondelmonti</a>. After Losha's death in 1374 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a> ruled both despotates until 1399, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muriq-shpata">Muriq Shpata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-shpata">Yaqub Shpata</a>, who allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-shpata">Yaqub Shpata</a> was killed in 1416, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, captured Arta. The city was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1449.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/despotate-of-arta.jpg" alt="Despotate of Arta" width="320" height="261"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Despotate_of_Arta,_1390.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1358 CE – 1416 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter Losha, Gjin Bua Shpata, Muriq Shpata</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian; Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Albanian Despotate of Arta, Despotate of Arta (Spata), Spata despotate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1387&amp;focus=despotate-of-arta" aria-label="Despotate of Arta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5199 774 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Despotate of Arta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5519 1030L5519 1030"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5519 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5519 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5519 1030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arta. <a href="/atlas?year=1387&amp;focus=despotate-of-arta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
